This document contains testing guidelines, modification rules, and
conventions for contributing to the system deployment scripts.

--- Testing the Scripts ------------------------------------------------

Three layers, cheapest first. The point of all three is that they
assert; a harness that only shows you a console is a harness that
cannot catch a system which installs cleanly, boots cleanly, reports
no failed units, and is silently misconfigured anyway.

1. tests/lint.sh - static checks. No root, no disk, no VM, and it
   finishes in well under a second.

     ./tests/lint.sh           warn if shellcheck is absent
     ./tests/lint.sh --strict  fail if shellcheck is absent

   It runs shellcheck over every *.sh in the repository, then checks
   that every package name the scripts hand to pacman still exists,
   by extracting them from the pacstrap and `pacman --sync` lines and
   batching them through `pacman -Si`. Both profiles are covered in
   one run, because the FOO_PKG variables are resolved against every
   value they take, not just the current machine's.

   Whole-line comments are dropped before that match. They were not
   always, and a comment mentioning pacstrap or pacman parsed as an
   invocation, every word after the command name being checked as a
   package. A sentence explaining an install line is the likeliest
   place for that, which is exactly where it was found.

   Package names rot silently as upstream reorganises. This is the
   only check that catches that, and it is by far the cheapest.
   Run it before anything else. Note it is only as current as the
   local sync database, and says so when that database is stale.

   It then does the same for the two repositories post_install.sh
   delegates to, if their working copies are present under tmp/ (see
   layer 4 below). It checks that deploy.sh and the pkgbuilds
   install.sh are where post_install.sh probes for them, that both
   answer --list with something, and that every name in every
   PKGBUILD's depends and makedepends still resolves. That last one
   matters because makepkg pulls those at build time: a name that no
   longer exists fails the build an hour into a VM run rather than
   here, in a second.

   Findings from shellcheck in those two scripts are reported but not
   counted. They belong to repositories this one does not own, and a
   warning there should not fail this one's lint -- but post_install.sh
   executes them, so a break there still breaks a deployment.

2. tests/assertions.sh - the post-conditions, and the load-bearing
   piece. Sourceable, so the same claims can be checked against a
   tree mounted by the VM harness, or against a running machine:

     ./tests/assertions.sh /mnt amd     a freshly installed tree
     sudo ./tests/assertions.sh / amd   this machine, right now

   Five groups. `suite_baseline` is every promise install.sh already
   makes (subvolumes, mount options, cmdline tokens, the UKI, hooks,
   fstab, enabled units); these must pass, and a failure is a
   regression. `suite_fresh` is the handoff to post_install.sh, valid
   only on a just-installed tree. `suite_userland` is what the
   dotfiles and PKGBUILD repositories left behind. `suite_live` is
   what only a running kernel can answer. `suite_pending` is the
   changes PLAN.amd and PLAN.intel describe but that have not landed;
   those report as PEND, do not fail the run, and shrink to nothing as
   the two plans are implemented.

   `suite_baseline` asserts firmware per profile, and asserts one
   package is absent as well as which are present. That asymmetry is
   deliberate: linux-firmware depends on linux-firmware-intel, so a
   positive check stays green even if the whole vendor metapackage
   comes back. Only naming the package that must not be there catches
   the regression this profile actually had.

   `suite_userland` needs to know what was selected, since nothing on
   the finished system records it. tests/vm.sh exports the selections
   and this script reads the same two variables, so the run and the
   checks cannot disagree. By hand, pass them as arguments:

     sudo ./tests/assertions.sh / amd all st-git,dmenu-git

   Given nothing, it reports SKIP: no selection was made, so nothing
   was proven. What it checks when given something is derived from the
   repository rather than listed here -- every regular file in a stow
   package must be a symlink in the home directory pointing back at
   it, so a dotfile added upstream is checked the day it lands. The
   two temporary sudoers rules are checked unconditionally: both grant
   the user passwordless access to a binary that is root by another
   name, and the case where one survives is exactly the case where the
   run failed partway.

   Checks that need root to read (the ESP is dmask=0077, and listing
   Btrfs subvolumes is privileged) report SKIP rather than a
   misleading failure when run unprivileged.

3. tests/vm.sh - a real UEFI boot, for the claims the first two
   cannot reach: that the UKI actually boots, that sd-boot works,
   that the LUKS volume unlocks, and that post_install.sh survives
   contact with a running system.

     ./tests/vm.sh --profile amd      both phases, headless, asserts
     ./tests/vm.sh --profile intel    the laptop's disk layout
     ./tests/vm.sh --phase install    phase one only
     ./tests/vm.sh --phase post       phase two only, needs a kept disk
     ./tests/vm.sh --interactive      a console, to poke by hand
     ./tests/vm.sh --keep             keep the disk image and journals
     ./tests/vm.sh --dotfiles LIST    what phase two stows
     ./tests/vm.sh --pkgbuilds LIST   what phase two compiles
     ./tests/vm.sh --userland DIR     where the two working copies are

   The target disk is sized per profile: 20G for amd, 28G for intel,
   which carves a 6 GiB hibernation swapfile out of it. qcow2 is
   sparse, so the larger number costs nothing until it is used.

   Journals are deleted at the start of every run and only then. That
   matters because --forward-journal appends and --keep leaves the
   work directory in place, so a run following a kept run used to
   replay the previous guest's transcript ahead of its own, printing
   two sets of assertions from two versions of the tree. The verdict
   was never affected -- the result markers are read last-wins -- but
   the log a human reads was, which is just as misleading. --keep
   still preserves the disk image, NVRAM and TPM state afterwards.

   LIST is comma-separated, or "all", or "none". The dotfiles default
   to all, because stowing is seconds of work and the conflict
   handling is the interesting part. The PKGBUILDs default to
   dmenu-git,st-git rather than all, because all means compiling Emacs
   and llama.cpp inside a VM; two suckless builds exercise the same
   makepkg path -- the temporary sudoers rule, PKGDEST, the --syncdeps
   dependency pull -- in minutes instead of hours.

   Phase one boots the ISO, runs install.sh against a blank virtual
   disk, asserts on the mounted tree, and checks that `umount -R /mnt`
   actually succeeds. Phase two then boots THAT disk for real and runs
   post_install.sh on it, followed by the full assertion set against a
   live root. Phase two is the only thing that exercises the UKI,
   sd-boot, the LUKS unlock, and every service post_install.sh enables.

   The root filesystem is encrypted, so phase two would normally stop
   at a passphrase prompt. It does not, because vmspawn passes the
   `cryptsetup.passphrase` credential and systemd-cryptsetup in the
   initrd reads it. Note this works for a volume named by
   `rd.luks.name=` even though systemd-cryptsetup(8) documents the
   credential against the gpt-auto-generated unit; it was verified
   here rather than assumed.

   Nothing here runs as root on the host. The installer runs inside
   the guest, which is the whole point: install.sh calls
   `cryptsetup open ... cryptroot`, `swapoff --all`, and reflector
   with `--save /etc/pacman.d/mirrorlist`. On a host whose own root
   is already /dev/mapper/cryptroot those are, in order, a name
   collision, your swap, and your mirrorlist. A VM has none of those
   problems, so install.sh runs there exactly as it does on real
   hardware, with no test-only branches to weaken the result.

   The disk type is chosen to match the profile, which is not
   cosmetic: amd gets an NVMe (/dev/nvme0n1) and intel a SCSI disk
   (/dev/sda), so the two runs exercise both branches of the
   partition-suffix logic in install.sh.

   systemd-vmspawn supplies as flags what the retired test_env.sh
   assembled by hand: OVMF discovery and a private NVRAM copy, swtpm
   started and torn down for us, the repository shared in over
   virtiofs, and the guest journal forwarded to the host. It is a
   front end to QEMU, not a replacement for it, so qemu-base and
   swtpm must be installed; edk2-ovmf and virtiofsd arrive as
   dependencies.

   Automation needs no custom ISO. vmspawn passes two credentials
   over SMBIOS, and the stock ISO's own systemd acts on them:
   systemd.extra-unit.* adds a service that runs the installer and
   then the assertions, and systemd.unit-dropin.multi-user.target
   pulls that service into the boot. The ISO is cached under
   tests/.cache and checksummed against upstream before it is booted.

   The scripts no longer redirect their own output to /dev/ttyS0. That
   block existed only because the retired test_env.sh pointed QEMU's
   serial port at a file, and under vmspawn it does worse than
   nothing: there is no serial sink, the write fails, and the
   installer dies with "sed: couldn't flush stdout". Under this
   harness the guest journal is the transcript. On real hardware,
   ask for one at invocation time instead:

     script -c './install.sh /dev/nvme0n1 amd' /tmp/install.log

   ARCH_BOOTSTRAP_UNATTENDED=1 is what makes both scripts drivable,
   and is never set on real hardware.

     install.sh       needs ARCH_BOOTSTRAP_PASSPHRASE. Skips the
                      destructive-format confirmation, reads the LUKS
                      passphrase from the environment rather than the
                      terminal, leaves root locked, and passes
                      --variables=no to bootctl so a loop-mounted or
                      virtual ESP cannot write the host's EFI
                      variables.
     post_install.sh  needs ARCH_BOOTSTRAP_PASSWORD. Skips the dotfile
                      and PKGBUILD selection prompts, answering them
                      from ARCH_BOOTSTRAP_DOTFILE_SELECTION and
                      ARCH_BOOTSTRAP_PKGBUILD_SELECTION (empty, and it
                      picks nothing), and sets a throwaway user
                      password instead of asking. See the section on
                      the userland working copies above.

   Two host-side details worth knowing if you change the harness.
   Results travel out of the guest in the forwarded journal, not over
   SSH, so --pass-ssh-key=no is set deliberately: leaving it on makes
   vmspawn generate a throwaway key every boot, and that is a step
   that can fail for no benefit. And the repository reaches the guest
   as a small ext4 image built with `mkfs.ext4 -d`, not as a virtiofs
   --bind, because --bind needs virtiofsd to map a UID range into a
   user namespace: unprivileged that requires systemd-nsresourced,
   whose socket ships disabled, and vmspawn rejects --private-users=
   for anything but --directory= boots.

--- The Userland Working Copies (tmp/) ---------------------------------

post_install.sh ends by handing the last two steps of a deployment to
two other repositories: dotfiles, whose deploy.sh stows the config
files, and pkgbuilds, whose install.sh compiles the custom packages.
Left alone it clones both from GitHub over https.

Clone them into tmp/ instead and the harness uses those working copies:

  git clone <dotfiles>  tmp/dotfiles
  git clone <pkgbuilds> tmp/pkgbuilds

tests/vm.sh then stages them into the payload image, and post_install.sh
copies from there rather than cloning. Three things follow. A test run
exercises the trees on this machine, uncommitted work included, instead
of whatever the remote is serving. A run no longer depends on the
remote being reachable from inside the guest at exactly the wrong
moment. And tests/lint.sh can check them, which is where the PKGBUILD
dependency check above comes from.

They are copied, not cloned, and the history is stripped on the way in.
git refuses to clone from a repository whose ownership it does not
recognise, and the staged tree belongs to whoever built the image; a
copy sidesteps that without teaching post_install.sh a test-only
exception. tmp/ is gitignored.

Without the working copies everything still runs -- the guest clones,
as it does on real hardware, and lint.sh says it is skipping that
section. It is simply a less faithful run.

Four environment variables drive this, all of them unset on real
hardware, where the prompts are the entire point of running the script
by hand:

  ARCH_BOOTSTRAP_DOTFILES_SRC        clone from here instead of the
  ARCH_BOOTSTRAP_PKGBUILDS_SRC       remote; a local path is copied
  ARCH_BOOTSTRAP_DOTFILE_SELECTION   what unattended mode picks,
  ARCH_BOOTSTRAP_PKGBUILD_SELECTION  instead of picking nothing

The selections are passed through unvalidated on purpose. deploy.sh
and the pkgbuilds install.sh already parse a selection and warn about
names they do not recognise; checking it again in post_install.sh
would test that copy of the logic rather than the one that runs.

Until this existed, unattended mode selected nothing, which meant the
two most failure-prone steps in post_install.sh -- stow's conflict
handling and a real makepkg build -- were the only ones no test ever
ran. They are also the two most likely to break, because they depend
on repositories that change independently of this one.

--- Modifying the Deployment Scripts -----------------------------------

install.sh (Base System)
  The script dynamically handles different disk nomenclatures
  (NVMe vs SATA). Changes to the `sfdisk` layout require updating
  the PART_EFI and PART_ROOT variables.

  Partitioning strictly assumes a UEFI + LUKS2 + Btrfs topology.

  The script now requires a mandatory hardware profile argument 
  (`amd` or `intel`). Microcode, KMS modules (amdgpu/i915), and 
  CPU scaling parameters are injected dynamically into the chroot.

  Everything that differs between the two machines is set in the
  single `case` block near the top, and nowhere else. Adding a
  per-profile knob means adding a variable there, not adding an `if`
  further down. The block currently carries UCODE_PKG, FW_PKG,
  NET_PKG, KMS_MODULE, PSTATE_FLAG, MOUNT_OPTS, EXTRA_CMDLINE and
  SWAPFILE_SIZE.

  MOUNT_OPTS is per profile because the desktop is an NVMe SSD and
  the laptop is a 5400 RPM disk; see README for the reasoning. Note
  that Btrfs applies mount options per FILESYSTEM, not per subvolume,
  so every subvolume mount inherits the same compression and commit
  settings. A per-subvolume option is silently ignored, which is why
  the swapfile's NOCOW guarantee comes from the file attribute that
  `btrfs filesystem mkswapfile` sets, not from a nodatacow mount.

  SWAPFILE_SIZE empty means no @swap subvolume and no swapfile at
  all; that is how the desktop opts out of hibernation. When it is
  set, the swapfile must be activated with swapon before genfstab
  runs, because genfstab reads /proc/swaps and would otherwise emit
  an fstab with no swap line.

  EXTRA_CMDLINE is appended to the kernel command line after the
  shared tokens. zswap.enabled=0 is deliberately NOT in it: it is
  shared by both profiles and lives in the base string.

  Chroot operations are isolated via heredoc (`<<'CHROOT'`).
  Required environment variables are explicitly injected into the
  `env` command to prevent premature expansion by the host shell.

  The kernel is packaged as a secure Unified Kernel Image (UKI) via
  mkinitcpio and systemd-ukify, not as separate vmlinuz/initramfs
  files.

post_install.sh (Post-installation)
  The script reads the `/root/.deploy_profile` state file generated
  in Phase 1 to apply the correct graphics packages (Vulkan/VA-API),
  restrict makepkg compilation threads on low-RAM systems, and select
  the CPU scaling policy.

  As in install.sh, per-profile values belong in the `case` block at
  the top: GPU_PKGS, VAAPI_ENV, MAKE_THREADS, CPU_GOVERNOR, CPU_EPP,
  ZRAM_SIZE, ZRAM_RESIDENT, PROFILE_PKGS, XKB_LAYOUT, XKB_VARIANT.

  CPU scaling is written to /etc/default/cpupower-service.conf with
  an UPPERCASE GOVERNOR. This is not interchangeable with
  /etc/default/cpupower: cpupower.service reads only the former, via
  `EnvironmentFile=-`, and /usr/lib/systemd/scripts/cpupower reads
  only $GOVERNOR. Writing the wrong file or the wrong case is a
  silent no-op that still leaves the unit enabled and exiting 0, so
  `systemctl --failed` will never reveal it. Verify by reading
  /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu0/cpufreq/scaling_governor directly.

  On `intel` the governor must be `powersave`, not `schedutil`.
  Tiger Lake has HWP, so intel_pstate runs in active mode, where the
  only accepted governors are powersave and performance. Bias is
  steered by CPU_EPP, which the same file carries.

  The laptop-only power management block is a single `if` near the
  end of the tuning section, writing explicit config files rather
  than adopting TLP, so every setting stays reviewable in this
  repository. The battery charge cap is feature-detected, because the
  in-kernel samsung-galaxybook driver probes per model.

  Interactive prompts (e.g., dotfile selection) are handled via
  local Bash namerefs (`local -n`), and read (I/O) is forced
  directly from `/dev/tty`, so a prompt is never lost when the
  script's own output is being piped or captured.

  Secure Boot integration (sbctl) attempts to automatically create
  and sign keys ONLY if the UEFI firmware reports being in
  "Setup Mode". It signs both UKIs, systemd-bootx64.efi, and
  /boot/EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI. That last one is the removable-media
  fallback the firmware uses when no NVRAM entry resolves, after an
  NVRAM reset or a CMOS clear; unsigned, it fails on a boot where
  nothing was changed, which is a miserable thing to debug.

  When the firmware is not in Setup Mode the block skips, and the
  summary at the end of the run says so in full rather than printing
  the success text. It used to print "keys are already signed/enrolled"
  unconditionally, which is worse than silence: acting on it means
  enabling Secure Boot against an unsigned UKI, and the firmware then
  refuses to boot with no useful diagnostic.

  Enrolling on locked-down OEM firmware
  -------------------------------------
  Verified on a Samsung laptop, AMI firmware 5.19, 2026-08-29. The
  defaults there make every step of this invisible.

  1. Key management is HIDDEN until a BIOS supervisor password is set.
     Without one there is only "Secure Boot Control: On/Off", and no
     way to reach Setup Mode at all. Set the password first; the
     Secure Boot Configuration menu then appears.

  2. Set "Secure Boot Mode" to Custom and leave it there. Standard
     restores the factory keys and discards yours.

  3. Set "Factory Default Key Provisioning" to Disabled BEFORE
     deleting anything. While it is enabled the firmware re-provisions
     factory keys as soon as it finds itself in Setup Mode, so the PK
     deletion appears to succeed and is silently undone on reboot.

  4. Delete the PK only, via PK Management. Do NOT use "Delete All
     Secure Boot Variables": that also erases dbx, and sbctl cannot
     put it back -- `enroll-keys --partial` covers PK, KEK and db and
     has no dbx handling. Since enroll-keys --microsoft then trusts
     Microsoft's certificates, wiping dbx leaves the machine unable to
     reject anything Microsoft has revoked. Setup Mode only requires
     that no PK is enrolled, so deleting the PK alone is sufficient
     and keeps the revocation list intact.

  5. `sbctl enroll-keys` will fail on immutable efivarfs entries.
     Clear the flag narrowly, never across the whole directory:

       sudo chattr -i /sys/firmware/efi/efivars/{KEK,db}-*

     The kernel reapplies it on the next boot; nothing to undo. Note
     db-* does not match dbx-*, so the revocation list stays locked.

  6. This firmware accepts db and KEK from the OS but REFUSES the PK.
     Enrolling anything else leaves Setup Mode still enabled, which is
     the symptom: sbctl reports success, `sbctl status` disagrees. The
     PK has to be enrolled from the firmware's own menu:

       cd /boot && sudo sbctl enroll-keys --microsoft --export auth

     /boot is the ESP, so it is FAT and the BIOS file browser can read
     it. Then PK Management -> Set New Key -> "authenticated variable"
     -> PK.auth. If the picker only offers "public key certificate",
     convert the key instead:

       sudo openssl x509 -in /var/lib/sbctl/keys/PK/PK.pem \
                         -outform DER -out /boot/PK.der

     Setup Mode reads Disabled once the PK takes. Remove the exported
     files from the ESP afterwards.

  7. Only then enable Secure Boot. tpm2-enroll.service fires on that
     boot and asks for the passphrase and a new PIN.

  8. If the supervisor password is now demanded at every boot, look
     for a separate "Password on boot" toggle and disable that. Do not
     clear the supervisor password itself: on this firmware it is what
     exposes the Secure Boot menu, and clearing it can revert Secure
     Boot Mode to Standard, which discards the keys and breaks the PIN
     along with them, since PCR 7 measures Secure Boot state.

  TPM 2.0 auto-unlock is NOT enrolled inline. Sealing a LUKS key to
  PCR 7 is only valid while Secure Boot is actively enforcing, which
  does not happen until after a reboot (and often a manual UEFI
  toggle). The script therefore installs `/usr/local/sbin/tpm2-enroll.sh`
  plus a `tpm2-enroll.service` one-shot unit gated by the marker
  `/var/lib/tpm2-enroll.pending`. The unit is ordered Before getty@tty1
  so the passphrase and PIN prompts own the console; once it exits,
  getty starts and offers a login. (Conflicts=getty@tty1 must not be
  used here: it cancels getty's start job for the whole boot, leaving
  tty1 without a login prompt after enrollment finishes.)
  It waits for `bootctl status` to report Secure Boot enabled,
  enrolls PCR 7 with `--tpm2-with-pin=yes --wipe-slot=tpm2` (idempotent
  and self-healing), removes the marker, and disables itself. If Secure
  Boot is still off it exits cleanly and retries on the next boot.

  Package compilation (makepkg) does not allow root execution.
  The script bypasses this by creating temporary atomic rules in
  sudoers (`zz-makepkg-pacman`), granting passwordless permission
  to pacman during compilation, and revoking access via `trap` at
  the end of execution.

  Dotfiles are not installed directly here; responsibility is
  delegated to the `deploy.sh` script (GNU Stow) under the context
  of the unprivileged user.

